5. Project 1 – Help Desk Ticket Management System
5.1 Project Overview
The Help Desk Ticket Management System is a web-based application developed using ASP.NET Core MVC. The purpose of the application is to provide a centralized platform for managing support requests and technical issues.
Users can create support tickets by submitting details about their problems. Support staff or administrators can then review, assign, prioritize, update, and resolve these tickets.
The system provides an organized workflow for handling support requests and helps improve communication between users and support teams.
________________________________________
5.2 Key Features
User Authentication and Authorization
The system can provide secure access to users and administrators through authentication and authorization mechanisms.
Different users can be provided with appropriate permissions based on their roles.
________________________________________
Ticket Creation
Users can create new support tickets by providing information such as:
•	Ticket title
•	Issue description
•	Category
•	Priority
•	User information
•	Date and time
This allows support teams to receive structured information about reported problems.
________________________________________
Ticket Management
Support staff can view and manage all submitted tickets.
They can:
•	View ticket details
•	Update ticket information
•	Change ticket status
•	Assign tickets
•	Set priority
•	Categorize issues
•	Resolve tickets
________________________________________
Ticket Assignment
Tickets can be assigned to specific support staff members based on their expertise or availability.
This ensures that issues are directed to the appropriate person for resolution.
________________________________________
Ticket Priority
Tickets can be organized according to their importance.
Typical priority levels include:
•	Low
•	Medium
•	High
•	Critical
Priority management helps support teams handle urgent issues first.
________________________________________
Ticket Status Tracking
The system maintains the current status of every ticket.
A typical workflow is:
New
 │
 ▼
Pending
 │
 ▼
Assigned
 │
 ▼
In Progress
 │
 ▼
Resolved
 │
 ▼
Closed
This allows users and support staff to monitor the progress of each issue.
________________________________________
Search and Filtering
The system can provide search and filtering functionality to help users quickly find specific tickets.
Tickets can be filtered based on:
•	Status
•	Priority
•	Category
•	Assigned staff
•	Ticket ID
•	Date
________________________________________
Ticket History
The system maintains information about support requests and their progress.
This helps organizations track previous issues and understand the complete lifecycle of a ticket.
________________________________________
5.3 MVC Architecture
The application follows the Model-View-Controller architecture.
             User
               │
               ▼
             View
          Razor Pages
               │
               ▼
          Controller
          C# / MVC
               │
               ▼
             Model
               │
               ▼
     Entity Framework Core
               │
               ▼
        SQL Server
Model
The Model represents application data and business entities.
Examples include:
•	User
•	Ticket
•	Category
•	Priority
•	Status
View
The View represents the user interface presented to the user.
The application uses Razor Views along with HTML, CSS, and Bootstrap.
Controller
The Controller handles incoming HTTP requests and coordinates communication between Views, Models, and the database.
________________________________________
5.4 Database Integration
The application uses Microsoft SQL Server as the database management system.
Entity Framework Core is used to interact with the database.
The database may contain entities such as:
Users
Tickets
Categories
Priorities
Statuses
TicketAssignments
TicketHistory
Entity Framework Core allows the application to perform operations without writing extensive SQL queries manually.
________________________________________
6. Project 2 – ASP.NET Core Web API
6.1 Project Overview
The ASP.NET Core Web API project demonstrates the development of a RESTful backend service using ASP.NET Core.
The API provides HTTP endpoints that allow external applications to communicate with the server and perform operations on application data.
The API can be consumed by:
•	Web applications
•	Mobile applications
•	Desktop applications
•	Other backend services
•	Third-party applications
________________________________________
6.2 Key Features
RESTful Architecture
The API follows REST principles and uses standard HTTP methods to perform operations.
GET     → Retrieve data
POST    → Create data
PUT     → Update data
DELETE  → Delete data
________________________________________
CRUD Operations
The API supports the four fundamental CRUD operations:
Create
Creates a new record using a POST request.
Read
Retrieves existing records using GET requests.
Update
Updates an existing record using a PUT request.
Delete
Removes a record using a DELETE request.
________________________________________
6.3 Example API Endpoints
HTTP Method	Endpoint	Function
GET	/api/Example	Retrieve all records
GET	/api/Example/{id}	Retrieve a record by ID
POST	/api/Example	Create a new record
PUT	/api/Example/{id}	Update an existing record
DELETE	/api/Example/{id}	Delete a record
________________________________________
6.4 API Architecture
The general architecture of the Web API is:
Client Application
       │
       │ HTTP Request
       ▼
API Controller
       │
       ▼
Service / Business Logic
       │
       ▼
Entity Framework Core
       │
       ▼
SQL Server Database
       │
       ▼
HTTP Response
       │
       ▼
Client Application
The Controller receives HTTP requests and processes them using application logic and database services.
________________________________________
6.5 JSON Data Exchange
The API uses JSON as the primary data exchange format.
Example response:
{
  "id": 1,
  "title": "Login Issue",
  "status": "In Progress",
  "priority": "High"
}
JSON provides a lightweight and standardized format for communication between the API and client applications.
________________________________________
6.6 Swagger / OpenAPI
The project uses Swagger/OpenAPI for API documentation and testing.
Swagger provides an interactive interface where developers can:
•	View API endpoints
•	View request parameters
•	Send API requests
•	Test CRUD operations
•	View API responses
•	Understand available endpoints
This simplifies API development and makes the service easier to consume.
________________________________________
7. Project 3 – xUnit Test Project
7.1 Project Overview
The xUnit Test Project is designed to perform automated unit testing of application components.
Unit testing helps verify that individual methods and components behave as expected.
The testing project can be used with:
•	ASP.NET Core MVC applications
•	ASP.NET Core Web APIs
•	Business logic
•	Services
•	Utility classes
________________________________________
7.2 Key Features
•	Automated unit testing
•	Test-driven validation
•	Independent test cases
•	Repeatable test execution
•	Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
•	[Fact] test methods
•	[Theory] parameterized tests
•	[InlineData] test data
•	Visual Studio Test Explorer support
•	.NET CLI test execution
________________________________________
7.3 Arrange-Act-Assert Pattern
The tests follow the AAA pattern.
Arrange
   │
   │ Prepare test data
   ▼
Act
   │
   │ Execute the method
   ▼
Assert
   │
   │ Verify expected result
   ▼
Test Passed / Failed
Arrange
The required objects and test data are prepared.
Act
The method or functionality being tested is executed.
Assert
The actual result is compared with the expected result.
________________________________________
7.4 Example xUnit Test
[Fact]
public void Add_ShouldReturnCorrectResult()
{
    // Arrange
    var calculator = new Calculator();

    // Act
    var result = calculator.Add(10, 20);

    // Assert
    Assert.Equal(30, result);
}
This test verifies that the Add() method correctly returns the expected result.
________________________________________
7.5 Common xUnit Attributes
Attribute	Purpose
[Fact]	Defines a standard unit test
[Theory]	Defines a parameterized test
[InlineData]	Supplies data to a theory
IClassFixture<T>	Provides shared test fixtures
________________________________________
7.6 Running Tests
Tests can be executed using the .NET CLI:
dotnet test
Detailed output can be generated using:
dotnet test --logger "console;verbosity=detailed"
Tests can also be executed through Visual Studio Test Explorer.
________________________________________
